{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And He blessed and He sanctified it</b> — He blessed it in the manna and sanctified it in the manna, and the verse is written concerning the future. This is the language of our Master Solomon in Bereshit Rabbah (Genesis Rabbah 11:2). And in the name of the Gaon Rav Saadiah they said that the blessing and the sanctification apply to those who observe it, that they will be blessed and sanctified. But the verse does not imply that it speaks of the future. And Rabbi Abraham said (Ibn Ezra on Genesis 2:3) that the blessing is an addition of good: that bodies will be renewed with an increase of strength in their offspring, and the soul with an increase of intelligence, and 'He sanctified it' — that no work was done in it as on the other days. And his words on this matter are correct for those who believe it, for this is not perceptible to people through the senses. But the truth is that the blessing in the day of Shabbat is the archetype of all blessings, and it is the foundation of the world, and 'He sanctified it,' because holiness flows from it. And if you understand this matter, you will know what they said in Bereshit Rabbah (Genesis Rabbah 11:8), because it has no pair, and what they further said: 'Let the Congregation of Israel be your pair,' and you will understand that on Shabbat there is truly an additional soul:" "<b>Which Elohim created to do</b> — work that was fit to be done on Shabbat, He doubled and did on the sixth, as is explained in Bereshit Rabbah (Genesis Rabbah 11:9); this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 2:3). But Rabbi Abraham said (Ibn Ezra on Genesis 2:3), according to its plain sense, that 'His work' — the roots in all the species, that He placed in them the power to produce after their kind. And it seems to me the correct interpretation is that He rested from all His work which He created from nothing, to make from it all the deeds mentioned in the six days. And so it says that He rested from creation and from works of the creation that He created on the first day and from the work that He did on the remaining days. And it is possible that 'to do' (לַעֲשׂוֹת) connects upward, as: in it He rested from all His work which He created from doing — and so too 'for they ceased counting' (Genesis 41:49), 'and they ceased building the city' (Genesis 11:8), 'take care not to ascend the mountain' (Exodus 19:12), 'and they did not depart from the king's command' (II Chronicles 8:15), and many similar. And know that there is further included in the word 'to do' (לַעֲשׂוֹת) that the six days of Creation are all the days of the world, for its duration will be six thousand years (Rosh HaShanah 31a), for which reason they said (Genesis Rabbah 19:8): 'A day of the Holy One, blessed be He, is a thousand years.' Now in the first two days the entire world was water and nothing was completed in them, and they are an allusion to the first two thousand years in which there was no one calling in the name of YHWH, and thus they said (Avodah Zarah 9a): 'Two thousand years of tohu.' But the creation on the first day was light, corresponding to the thousand years of the days of man who was the light of the world, recognizing his Creator — and perhaps mankind did not practice idolatry until the first man died. On the second day, 'let there be a firmament' and 'let it divide,' for in it the righteous Noah and his sons were separated from the wicked who were judged by water. On the third day the dry land appeared and sprouted and produced fruit — this is the third millennium, which begins when Abraham was forty-eight years old (Genesis Rabbah 64:4), and then 'he began to call in the name of YHWH' (Genesis 4:26), and a planting of a righteous one sprouted, for he drew many to know YHWH, as they expounded: 'and the souls that they made in Haran' (Genesis 12:5), and he commanded his household and his sons after him, and they kept the way of YHWH to do righteousness and justice (Genesis 18:19), and the matter ascended until his descendants received the Torah at Sinai and the Temple was built on that day, and then all the commandments were fulfilled, for they are the fruits of the world. And know that from the time of twilight it is already reckoned as the following day, and therefore the matter of each day begins slightly before it, as Abraham was born in the second millennium — and so you will find in each and every day. And the fourth day, in which the great luminary and the small luminary and the stars were created — its day alludes to the fourth millennium, which began from the time the First Temple was built seventy-two years after its construction until after the Second Temple, one hundred and seventy-two years. Now on this day all the children of Israel had light, for the glory of YHWH filled the House of YHWH, and Israel's fire was upon the altar, crouching there like a lion consuming the offerings, and afterward He diminished their light and they were exiled, as when the moon is at its conjunction, and it shone for them all the days of the Second Temple, and the fire upon the altar crouched like a dog, and both luminaries set on the evening of that day and the Temple was destroyed. On the fifth day the waters swarmed with living creatures and birds flew over the earth — an allusion to the fifth millennium, which begins one hundred and seventy-two years after the destruction of the Temple, for in it the nations will rule and man will be made like the fish of the sea, like creeping things that have no ruler over them — all of it he brings up with a hook, he gathers them in his dragnet and collects them in his net, and none seek YHWH. On the sixth day, in the morning, 'the earth brought forth living creatures after their kind, cattle and creeping things and beasts of the earth after their kind,' and their creation was before the sun rose, as it is written (Psalms 104:22): 'The sun rises, they gather, and in their dens they crouch,' and then man was created in the image of Elohim — and this is the time of his dominion, as it is said (Psalms 104:23): 'Man goes out to his work and to his labor until evening,' and this is the sixth millennium, for at its beginning the beasts will rule over it — these are the kingdoms that did not know YHWH — and after its tenth part, according to the measure of the sun's rising on the day (cf. Pesachim 94a), the redeemer will come, of whom it is said (Psalms 89:37): 'And his throne like the sun before me' — this is the son of David, made in the image of Elohim, as it is written (Daniel 7:13–14): 'And behold, with the clouds of heaven one like a son of man was coming, and to the Ancient of Days he arrived, and they brought him before Him, and to him was given dominion and glory and kingdom.' And this will be 118 years after five thousand years, to complete the matter from the mouth of Daniel (Daniel 12:11): 'And from the time the daily offering is removed and the abomination of desolation is given, one thousand two hundred and ninety days.' And it appears from the change of the days from the swarming things of the waters and the birds to the beasts of the earth, that at the beginning of the sixth millennium the kingdom of a ruling nation will be renewed, fearful and exceedingly mighty, approaching the truth more than the earlier ones. The seventh day, Shabbat, alludes to the World to Come, which is entirely Shabbat and rest for the life of the worlds. And may El preserve us in all the days, and set our portion with His perfect servants."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {0 "Resolved הגאון רב סעדיה as Saadiah Gaon based on the epithet and context; resolved רבינו שלמה as Solomon (likely R. Solomon bar Isaac = Rashi) per the Bereshit Rabbah attribution.", 1 "The figure '118 years after five thousand years' (קי\"ח אחר חמשת אלפים) is translated hyper-literally; the precise calendrical logic is cryptic and possibly textually difficult, as it appears to combine Daniel's 1290-day figure with a separate calculation in ways that are not self-evident. The phrase 'עד אחרי הבית השני קע\"ב שנה' (until after the Second Temple, 172 years) is likewise translated literally though its exact referent is ambiguous. Talmudic tractate citations in Aramaic abbreviations (e.g., ר\"ה = Rosh HaShanah, ע\"ז = Avodah Zarah, פסחים) have been resolved by standard reference."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:21:12.217082873Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}